<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Italian East Africa</h1><p class="st-meta">1936 CE – 1941 CE · capital Addis Ababa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Mussolini's short-lived East African empire, created after the conquest of Ethiopia and destroyed by British and Ethiopian forces in 1941.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> invaded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in October 1935, using aircraft and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poison-gas">poison gas</a>, and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> in May 1936. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-iii">Victor Emmanuel III</a> was proclaimed Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, and Ethiopia was merged with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/italian-eritrea">Italian Eritrea</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/italian-somaliland">Italian Somaliland</a> to form Italian East Africa, governed by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> in Addis Ababa.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The empire was divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">governorates</a>. Italian rule was harsh: after an attempt on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rodolfo-graziani">Rodolfo Graziani's</a> life in February 1937, thousands were massacred in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>, and the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debre-libanos">Debre Libanos</a> was attacked. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> built roads and planned settlement, but Ethiopian patriots, the arbegnoch, kept up resistance. In 1940 Italian forces occupied <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-somaliland">British Somaliland</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1941 British, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-nations">Commonwealth</a> and Ethiopian forces counter-attacked. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> re-entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> on 5 May 1941, and the last Italian forces surrendered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a> in November. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-eritrea">Eritrea</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/somalia">Somalia</a> passed under British administration.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/italian-east-africa.jpg" alt="Italian East Africa" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: F l a n k e r · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Italy_(1861-1946)_crowned.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1936 CE – 1941 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Addis Ababa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Victor Emmanuel III, Rodolfo Graziani, Amedeo, Duke of Aosta</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ethiopian Orthodox Christianity, Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian, Amharic, Tigrinya, Somali</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, <a href="/countries/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, <a href="/countries/somalia">Somalia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Africa Orientale Italiana, AOI</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1938&amp;focus=italian-east-africa" aria-label="Italian East Africa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5750 1840 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Italian East Africa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6070 2096L6140 2002M6070 2096L6063 1863M6070 2096L6259 2357M6070 2096L6173 1949M6070 2096L6029 1964M6070 2096L6240 2044"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 2002h0M6063 1863h0M6259 2357h0M6173 1949h0M6029 1964h0M6240 2044h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6070 2096h0"/><path c